WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.320
How quickly the misery and destruction of war&nbsp;
has spread across Ukraine as Russia seeks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.160
impose its brutal will on the country. But is&nbsp;
the conflict now entering its most critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:17.680
phase? The question to Lieutenant General&nbsp;
Ben Hodges, former commander, US Army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.320
Europe and my guest this week from Frankfurt.
This is going to be — that's why I mean it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.720
to be a decisive week. A decisive week ahead on&nbsp;
whether or not we can bring — force the Russians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.800
to culminate so that they can no longer attack.
The targeting of civilians and residential areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.400
prompted General Hodges to call for NATO&nbsp;
to impose a no-fly zone across Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.480
but the alliance said no. Is this weakness&nbsp;
or wise tactics? And what if Moscow is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.240
continuing threats to use nuclear weapons.
So it's almost like we are talking ourselves out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:01.200
of taking more forceful actions that we should.
All that and much more on Conflict Zone.&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.840
Lieutenant General Ben Hodges,&nbsp;
welcome to Conflict Zone.&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.640
Thank you.
Can we start with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.720
recent Russian attack on the Yavoriv military&nbsp;
base close to the Polish border with Ukraine?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.120
35 people died. This came after Russia had&nbsp;
warned that NATO weapons shipments to Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.400
would be seen as legitimate targets. We&nbsp;
don't, of course, know if some of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.800
shipments pass through that base. But looking at&nbsp;
the proximity of the attack to a NATO border, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.760
serious is that for the alliance, do you think?
Well, I'm more concerned about the impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:51.360
the facility at Yavoriv. This is a training center&nbsp;
that from the Soviet times that we, the U.S. Army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.520
and Canada, invested a lot of money over the&nbsp;
last six or seven years to turn it into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.840
meaningful training area, training&nbsp;
center for Ukrainian forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.440
And that's what it was for over the last&nbsp;
several years. I don't know that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.120
become a logistics hub or anything. I think the&nbsp;
Pentagon has been pretty close-mouthed about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.200
But I mean, going after, going after that base,&nbsp;
also is a demonstration that the Russians still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.520
have the capability to reach that far. And I&nbsp;
imagine they also intended this as a warning.&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.280
And a warning or an actual attempt to pull&nbsp;
NATO directly into the war? Do you think that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.800
do you think that's what they want to do?
No ... I do not think that they want to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.880
NATO into the war. That's, in fact, that's the&nbsp;
last thing that they want, is a situation where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:44.080
you've got all 30 nations-plus against Russia. I&nbsp;
think this is more about them trying to interdict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.720
the logistical lines of communication.
Do you think NATO will continue to supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.240
military assistance to Ukraine and will it&nbsp;
defend those shipments if necessary, directly?&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.400
For sure we're going to continue and in fact,&nbsp;
we've got to accelerate. We need to be doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:09.920
something on the scale of the Berlin Airlift,&nbsp;
with a sense of urgency. The next ten days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:18.480
are decisive, in my view. The next 10 days, if&nbsp;
we accelerate and expand the delivery of support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.400
I think the Russians actually are close to&nbsp;
culminating — to reaching the point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.680
they can no longer continue the attack, if we&nbsp;
keep the pressure on them for the next 10 days.&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.320
There's a lot of focus on China right now,&nbsp;
the US is claiming Russia has asked Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.840
for military help. Potentially a&nbsp;
huge development, that, isn't it.&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.160
Well, this is important.&nbsp;
The Chinese have to decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.400
what they want their role in the world to be. The&nbsp;
Russians obviously are in trouble. They know it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.360
Not only are they recruiting Syrians to&nbsp;
come join the fight because their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:01.600
manpower shortage is growing increasingly severe —&nbsp;
and they need financial help, which is why they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.120
turning to China, and I think they're looking for&nbsp;
some material support as well. So China really has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:14.720
an opportunity and an important decision to make.
But the possibility of an open military alliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.880
between the world's largest country&nbsp;
geographically and the world's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:23.840
populous nation — do you think NATO's even&nbsp;
considered such an alarming development?&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.240
Oh, there's no doubt the alliance&nbsp;
has been thinking about this. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:33.600
featured prominently over the past year in a lot&nbsp;
of the strategic thinking and the conversations.&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:37.360
Does it scare you?
No, because I just don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:43.760
think it's — I don't think it's going to happen.&nbsp;
Actually, there's not much advantage for China in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:52.880
being handcuffed to a Russia that is going to be&nbsp;
destitute and without a friend in the world. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.520
than energy for its own economy and easier access&nbsp;
to the Arctic, I don't see too many advantages for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:05.280
China here. This is bad for business for them.
General Hodges, on the ground in Ukraine, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.760
battle for Kyiv seems to be gathering momentum.&nbsp;
Is this going to decide the outcome of this war?&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:20.000
I think that the Russians are not going to be able&nbsp;
to encircle Kyiv, let alone capture it. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.240
look, for almost 19 days now they've been&nbsp;
trying to capture Mariupol. That should've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:31.360
been the easiest city for them to capture, given&nbsp;
the proximity of their navy and Sea of Azov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:38.960
forces there in Crimea and in Donbass, and yet&nbsp;
they still have not been able to capture Mariupol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.640
I don't believe that they're going to that&nbsp;
they have the ability to actually encircle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.160
Kyiv. I was there five weeks ago. I've&nbsp;
been there several times. This is a very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:54.640
very large city on the banks of one of the largest&nbsp;
rivers in Europe, very complex urban terrain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.200
They don't have the manpower to encircle it.
But the failure, if Russia failed to take Kyiv,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.520
that would be a catastrophic blow to them,&nbsp;
wouldn't it? Won't they have orders, won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:11.360
they have orders to keep on regardless, flatten&nbsp;
the city if necessary? Take it at all costs?&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.120
There's no doubt that they will continue&nbsp;
to launch rockets and missiles into Kyiv&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.480
the way they've done other Ukrainian cities,&nbsp;
which is why it's so important that the aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.080
that we are providing to Ukraine&nbsp;
include the capability to destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:32.480
the rocket launchers and missile launching&nbsp;
systems that Russians are using. But this is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.200
this is going to be — that's why I mean it's going&nbsp;
to be a decisive week. A decisive week ahead on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:43.520
whether or not we can bring — force the Russians&nbsp;
to culminate so that they can no longer attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:50.320
And then, Ukraine has a more advantageous position&nbsp;
for whatever sort of negotiations are happening.&nbsp;

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.760
You clearly want NATO to do more to help&nbsp;
Ukraine than it's been doing. Last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:01.520
you co-signed an open letter calling for the&nbsp;
alliance to impose a no-fly zone over Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.560
"to avert and deter Russian bombardment,"&nbsp;
you said, "more must be done to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:12.000
more wide-scale casualties and the potential&nbsp;
bloodbath." Do you feel NATO's been too soft?&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:19.600
I think that we are overreacting to Russian&nbsp;
threats about expansion of the war, about nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.960
weapons. I don't believe that the Kremlin will use&nbsp;
a nuclear weapon. I don't, you know, first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.640
Putin cannot do it by himself, and I don't believe&nbsp;
that the people around him are actually interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.600
in this becoming a nuclear threat. So it's almost&nbsp;
like we are talking ourselves out of taking more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:44.480
forceful actions that we should. We are talking&nbsp;
too much about what we can't do and what we won't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:52.320
do, and not enough about what we, what we can do.&nbsp;
Look Srebrenica, July 1995, European troops under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.840
a United Nations mandate, stood outside&nbsp;
the city, while Republika Srpska forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:04.880
murdered 8,000 Bosnian men and boys. I do not&nbsp;
want us to be a part of something like that again.&nbsp;

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:09.840
Your letter said NATO leaders should make&nbsp;
clear that they won't countenance Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.560
attacks on civilian areas, but they have&nbsp;
countenanced them, and they continue to do so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:24.560
And they turned down your proposal for the no-fly&nbsp;
zone. Does that concern you? That really they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:29.600
ceding the advantage to the Russians here.
You know, obviously, I'm not a very compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:37.200
writer. Yeah, I do feel like that. We are we have&nbsp;
lost some momentum. The first two weeks of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.240
I thought the White House was doing a terrific&nbsp;
job, the the allies sticking together and we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:49.280
still sticking together. But the last four days,&nbsp;
it feels like we began to become more risk averse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:57.280
more cautious, and the messaging was more about&nbsp;
no escalation and less about stopping the murder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:02.160
of thousands of Ukrainian, innocent Ukrainians.
But there's an awkward dilemma here for NATO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.640
isn't there? NATO has to do what's right for&nbsp;
NATO. It can't necessarily do what's right for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:11.840
Ukraine at the same time — the two&nbsp;
aren't necessarily the same are they?&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.920
I don't know that I accept the premise of the&nbsp;
question. You're right, Ukraine is not a NATO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.960
member, and the alliance exists for the&nbsp;
collective defense of all of its members.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:31.040
But if you accept the notion that what's happening&nbsp;
in Ukraine is not limited to Ukraine — and I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.600
mean a stray missile going over the border,&nbsp;
I'm talking about what Putin is doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:40.080
what is the impact on Europe, stability&nbsp;
and security in Europe, then I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.840
NATO has to take a broader view of this.
Joe Biden has repeatedly felt the need to state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:51.760
publicly that the US won't directly intervene&nbsp;
against Russian forces in Ukraine. He did so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:58.400
again last Friday. Is Biden pushing back against&nbsp;
domestic pressure for him to do more? And if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.080
would you suspect that pressure is&nbsp;
coming partly from inside the Pentagon?&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:08.080
Actually, the Pentagon is usually the most&nbsp;
conservative institution in Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.120
so I don't think he's getting too much pressure&nbsp;
from the Pentagon to do more. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.560
the Joint [Chiefs of] Staff and the Department&nbsp;
of Defense are doing what they should do:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:24.400
prudent planning, anticipation of requirements&nbsp;
and giving the president options. But I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:31.040
think he's getting pressure from the Pentagon. The&nbsp;
Congress seems to be in a bipartisan way in favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:37.840
of doing a lot more. I think that the&nbsp;
president has overstated this business about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.000
"No, we're not going to do this because it's&nbsp;
as if it's automatically going to be World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:48.080
Three." I don't know that I agree with that.
He — Biden — has delivered a strong warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.440
about chemical weapons, saying Russia would pay&nbsp;
a severe price if it used them. That's a that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.000
an easy threat to make. How seriously do you&nbsp;
take it coming from Biden? I mean, Biden was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:03.440
vice president when Barack Obama gave a similar&nbsp;
red line warning to Syria, which did use chemical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.080
weapons, but the warning never turned into&nbsp;
action. Could it be the same this time around?&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.520
I would think not. No. You're right that&nbsp;
President Biden was vice president during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:18.480
the Obama administration when this famous&nbsp;
red line was given to the Assad regime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.960
The Assad regime jumped right over it&nbsp;
with Russian help and the U.S., U.K.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.800
France, Germany did nothing. And I think that&nbsp;
emboldened the Russians to go ahead and be even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.800
more aggressive. So that's why President Biden&nbsp;
has not said this is a red line because that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:42.880
that figure of speech — you box yourself in. I&nbsp;
would anticipate, though, that there would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:48.800
consequences that would be even more severe for&nbsp;
the Kremlin, although we're running out of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:56.160
other things to do to them short of kinetic force.
But chemical weapons is part of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:03.440
warfare doctrine, isn't it? I mean, they've used&nbsp;
it specifically against their opposition figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:08.320
like Alexei Navalny and some of those that&nbsp;
they've attacked on foreign streets in Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:14.800
A former U.S. official at the National&nbsp;
Security Council says Moscow accepts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:19.760
the use of chemical agents in warfare. "The&nbsp;
stakes for Putin are existential," he said.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:24.880
"If that's one more tool he can throw this&nbsp;
thing, he will." Do you think that's possible?&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.120
Yeah. I agree with Fiona Hill's comments&nbsp;
a few weeks ago when she was asked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.160
this and she said, oh, yes, he would. That&nbsp;
doesn't mean he will, but certainly I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:42.880
he would countenance countless myths, even&nbsp;
if it was not for state survival. But if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:50.000
was necessary to accelerate the extermination of&nbsp;
Ukrainians or to depopulate the area by getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.520
Ukrainians on the road. The most likely scenario&nbsp;
with chemical weapons is probably something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:04.480
Syria — chlorine, not the more... not something&nbsp;
like a nerve agent. Chlorine still kills people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:12.160
but the casualty rate probably is somewhere&nbsp;
similar to rockets artillery, but it puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:17.760
things in a different category. And certainly,&nbsp;
you know, the idea of seeing kids and old people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:25.520
murdered by any kind of chemical weapon, any&nbsp;
kind of gas is going to put immense pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:30.320
on the administration and other heads of state&nbsp;
and government to take action. And it should.&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:36.320
And those who will be most worried about the&nbsp;
threat of chemical weapons would be Eastern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:42.080
Europe, Baltic states. They've been warning&nbsp;
about Russian aggression in the region for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:46.400
some time, the question is why nobody's been&nbsp;
listening to their voices over the years.&nbsp;

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:54.640
Well, I think that there is a large part of&nbsp;
Western Europe that is further away from Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.640 --> 00:14:02.320
but also inside the United States and inside&nbsp;
the UK, where you've got huge Russian money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:11.760
business, energy dependencies. So there's been a&nbsp;
reluctance to confront this for a variety of bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.960
reasons, and I would say shame on everybody that&nbsp;
said, "come on, Russia is not going to do this."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.000
I live in Frankfurt. I hear it from friends all&nbsp;
the time before this happened: "Come on, what are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:28.080
you worried about? It makes no sense. Putin would&nbsp;
never attack." And here we are. And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:34.320
in the real world, you can't make your&nbsp;
threats go away by looking the other way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.000
or wishing that they didn't exist. And&nbsp;
so we're paying the price for that now.&nbsp;

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:46.560
To date, NATO's reinforcements in those countries&nbsp;
don't have too much deterrent value, do they?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.120
A few thousand extra troops, a few more&nbsp;
fighter aircraft. Not nearly enough, is it?&nbsp;

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.240
No, first of all, I would&nbsp;
completely disagree with you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:59.360
Tim, that they don't have much deterrent&nbsp;
effect. It's been pretty effective so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:06.400
But I do agree with you that we don't have enough.&nbsp;
We don't have enough air and missile defense. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:12.960
don't have enough logistics. The ability to move&nbsp;
quickly across Europe is not what it needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:20.880
to really be a strong deterrent should Russian&nbsp;
forces move into Belarus in stronger numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:27.280
which I expect is going to happen thanks to this&nbsp;
bogus constitutional referendum in Belarus a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:34.640
weeks ago. Then this raises the risk for our&nbsp;
allies in Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:39.840
do need to improve air and missile defense. We&nbsp;
do need to improve the logistical preparations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:48.720
But I would also say, I think that the alliance&nbsp;
is now going to consider more favorably permanent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:54.640
basing in Romania and Poland and Baltic countries,&nbsp;
not just rotational forces. I would support that.&nbsp;

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.480
And what do you think the&nbsp;
reaction from Russia would be?&nbsp;

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:04.000
Of course they will, they will complain about&nbsp;
it, but in my view, they're in no position to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:12.320
complain. They've... we did not have permanent&nbsp;
basing in there since the NATO Russia Founding Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:19.120
because one of the provisions said no permanent&nbsp;
basing. And so we adhered to that. But when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:25.280
Russians abrogated the NATO Russia Founding Act,&nbsp;
with their attack in Georgia and then in Ukraine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:29.840
the alliance continued to live by that.&nbsp;
It's obvious that our good faith effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:35.520
has not been rewarded by good behavior by the&nbsp;
Russians. And so we need to replace that naiveté&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:42.640
with some serious deterrence measures.
You talked earlier about wanting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:47.520
stop Russia calling the shots, and&nbsp;
it's about time that the NATO alliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:55.280
called the shots. How should the West be setting&nbsp;
the agenda instead of responding to Moscow's?&nbsp;

00:16:56.720 --> 00:17:01.840
Well, first of all, they need to be feeling pain&nbsp;
immediately and in a lot of different places.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:07.440
The sanctions are important, the effects&nbsp;
are going to be felt for years to come.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:15.280
But as you know, sanctions take a while to really&nbsp;
start delivering effect. Some inconveniences for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:21.120
Russians now. But the real pain is going to be&nbsp;
felt in the coming months, to include, Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:29.120
probably will default on 150-billion dollar debt&nbsp;
on loans because their economy is in tatters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:33.120
Aeroflot will not be able to fly anymore because&nbsp;
they don't have repair parts. And so there's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:38.880
to be serious impact in the coming months. But&nbsp;
more immediately, we need to be doing two things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.440
in addition, obviously, to providing&nbsp;
aid to the Ukrainians that they need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:50.800
Number one, we should be trying to reach every&nbsp;
Russian family and tell them what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:54.960
There's a reason that the Kremlin has put a&nbsp;
media blackout all over Russia that social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.960 --> 00:18:00.080
media does not allow people to understand what's&nbsp;
happening. The older demographic in Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:04.880
of course, has completely bought into state-run&nbsp;
TV, but there is another part of the population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:09.360
I don't think so. There are still thousands of&nbsp;
Russians that have the courage to go out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:14.480
protest, the woman that we all saw on Russian TV&nbsp;
last night that jumped in behind the commentator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:21.280
with the sign saying "Stop the war". This&nbsp;
is there. On 1 April, on the 1st of April,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:27.120
130,000 Russian families are required&nbsp;
to send their son to be conscripts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:35.680
for the Russian army. If we could reach just&nbsp;
10 or 20 percent of that 130,000 and tell them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:41.120
to resist and not send their son, I think that&nbsp;
would send a shockwave across Russia, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:47.760
could really generate more domestic pressure.&nbsp;
That's hard to do, but I think this is doable.&nbsp;

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:54.960
General Hodges, you've been pretty scathing about&nbsp;
Europe's defense posture in the past. In your book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.960 --> 00:19:00.960
"Future War" you say that "Europe is finally&nbsp;
waking up from a 30-year strategic slumber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:07.760
but there's little unity of effort or purpose and&nbsp;
even less strategic direction." Any sign that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:11.280
changing in the last few days? The&nbsp;
idea that the EU would actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:16.480
ship weapons to Ukraine or anywhere else certainly&nbsp;
speaks to a sudden awakening, doesn't it?&nbsp;

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:23.680
Yeah. I would say it's been quite an awakening&nbsp;
and Germany has been the key here. When Chancellor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:29.440
Scholz made his speech about three weeks ago there&nbsp;
in front of the Bundestag, where he talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:35.840
2%, he talked about a hundred billion euros&nbsp;
and allowing weapons to move through Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:41.600
or weapons over which Germany had some control,&nbsp;
this was critical. That, to me, was the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:47.920
slamming shut on any hope that Putin might have&nbsp;
had to separate the alliance or to divide the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:53.760
from Europe. German leadership here is essential.&nbsp;
Now German....it's not just the economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:58.560
it's sort of the moral authority that Germany&nbsp;
has accrued over the last seven or eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:06.000
decades. And I think that German political&nbsp;
leadership, the so-called elites, realized that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:14.560
they were in danger of seeing that credibility&nbsp;
erode right underneath their feet. They could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:25.280
not claim to be the champion of European values&nbsp;
and of the "gesamte europäische Friedensordnung."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:31.920
The peace and stability culture of Europe,&nbsp;
if they still abide while Russia smashed it.&nbsp;

00:20:32.480 --> 00:20:37.760
What's what's alarming in your judgment is&nbsp;
the fact that while Russia is exploiting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:43.600
evolution in military technology, hypersonic&nbsp;
weapons, laser particle beam systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:49.520
Europe, you say, has chosen not to. How&nbsp;
serious is it that Europe should actually try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:54.960
to catch up with these advanced technologies?
Well, of course, if you want to defeat those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.960 --> 00:21:00.160
technologies or deter their use against you, you&nbsp;
have to demonstrate that you have the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:06.400
to do that. Now, stand fast the fact that the&nbsp;
Russians have not been able to actually employ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:11.040
very effectively much of these technologies. I&nbsp;
have been a little bit surprised that I have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:17.520
seen more cyber use. That I have not seen more&nbsp;
effective integration of their modern systems.&nbsp;

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:22.960
Did you overestimate their capabilities?
I did. I absolutely did. And I think as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:29.200
I look back now, the missing ingredient, it's&nbsp;
not, it's not the device, it's not the platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:34.160
it's the training and the culture of readiness.&nbsp;
And I think what we're seeing is the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:42.400
of decades of corruption and false reporting&nbsp;
inside the Russian defense industry and inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.320
the Ministry of Defense. A lot of money was&nbsp;
spent, but they didn't get what they paid for.&nbsp;

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:51.280
You said recently the Kremlin is&nbsp;
always in a war state of mind. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:59.840
NATO isn't, is it? And it hasn't been. Can you now&nbsp;
foresee NATO moving, excuse me, to the same kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:05.120
of war footing as Russia? Does it need to?
I think we're going to see more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:10.800
being more clear-eyed about the threat. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:16.400
you know, we probably, first of all, couldn't&nbsp;
believe that there would be a big war in Europe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:23.280
Secondly, we were accustomed to what war&nbsp;
looked like in Afghanistan and Iraq, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:29.200
with missiles flying into buildings and thousands&nbsp;
of people being killed and displaced like we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:38.480
seeing right now. So we have forgotten about the&nbsp;
lethality of high end of war. So I think there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.480 --> 00:22:43.040
grim realization that if we want to prevent it,&nbsp;
that you have to be, you have to be ready for it.&nbsp;

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:52.240
What comes next, in your view, as far as the&nbsp;
battles are concerned in Ukraine? If Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.880 --> 00:23:00.160
won't accept to lose, and can't be seen to lose&nbsp;
in the international arena, what do you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:08.160
he would do? Is the West on a knife edge here?
And I don't think so. I think we've we have taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:15.440
too much counsel of our own fears about a nuclear&nbsp;
conflict and allowed him to use threats to keep us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:20.800
from doing what needs to be done. I'm not saying&nbsp;
that there's no risk and obviously nobody I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:26.000
wants it to go that way. But I think we have&nbsp;
given him too much credit for the ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:32.480
do those things, and we've deterred ourself. What&nbsp;
comes next? If I'm right that within the next 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:37.680
10 to 12 days, Russian forces culminate,&nbsp;
they can no longer continue the attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:43.040
They will obviously want to hang on&nbsp;
to what they've got. This is when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:47.920
should be pouring it on. We'll have a better&nbsp;
negotiating position and we should demand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:55.040
they go back to not just pre-February 24, but&nbsp;
what President Biden that Ukrainian sovereignty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.920 --> 00:24:00.480
is a priority for the United States, that they&nbsp;
should leave Crimea, they should leave the Donbass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.600 --> 00:24:06.160
and then we get on with recovery. I mean, there's&nbsp;
going to be more than three million refugees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:12.400
that want to come home. This is going be a massive&nbsp;
problem to rebuild and help repatriate people. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:17.440
also, as you and your viewers know, there are&nbsp;
millions of people across Europe and the Middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:23.680
East that depend on grain from Ukraine for food.&nbsp;
We're going to have a serious food problem here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:28.720
over the next year until Ukraine is able&nbsp;
to start exporting agriculture again.&nbsp;

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:38.560
And the humanitarian emergency that's now a&nbsp;
very real, a very all-present reality for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:46.320
in the major cities. People are living in what&nbsp;
one aid agency called apocalyptic conditions. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:52.240
just stand by and watch that for the time being?
Oh, of course not. There are dozens and dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:58.480
of agencies that are out already trying to&nbsp;
help, particularly in Poland, where agencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:04.480
have moved there to help deal with refugees, and&nbsp;
some are working inside Ukraine. I know several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:10.720
people who are personally involved in this, but&nbsp;
there's also going to be a part of the cleanup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:17.200
as you know, from all your years as a journalist&nbsp;
in a conflict like this. There is so much what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:22.640
call UXO — unexploded ordnance — everywhere. The&nbsp;
cluster munitions that the Russians have used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:28.960
rounds that did not detonate. This stuff is&nbsp;
lying all over the battlefield everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.960 --> 00:25:35.200
And so as people go back in and try to clean up&nbsp;
these cities, this is going to be a real problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:42.160
for quite some time. These nuclear plants, I&nbsp;
mean, we've got some cleanup to do there as well.&nbsp;

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:44.720
All right, General Hodges, it's been&nbsp;
good to have you on Conflict Zone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.720 --> 00:26:00.880
Thanks very much indeed for your time.
Thank you. Thanks for the privilege, Tim.

